<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Oudenarde</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1708">1708</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Oudenarde · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Oudenarde was fought on 11 July 1708 on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scheldt">Scheldt</a> in the Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, where an allied army under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-churchill-1st-duke-of-marlborough">Duke of Marlborough</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-eugene-of-savoy">Prince Eugene of Savoy</a> defeated a French army under the Dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> and Vendôme. The victory let the allies recover the initiative in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> and besiege the fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lille">Lille</a>, which fell later that year.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-oudenarde.jpg" alt="Battle of Oudenarde" width="320" height="254" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Copy_after_Jan_van_Huchtenburgh_(Haarlem_1647-Amsterdam_1733)_-_The_Battle_of_Oudenarde,_1708._-_RCIN_404900_-_Royal_Collection.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a> followed the death of the childless <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-ii-of-spain">Charles II of Spain</a> in November 1700. He left his monarchy undivided to Philip, a grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>, and the Grand Alliance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a> backed Charles, younger son of Emperor Leopold I. After the French defeats at Blenheim and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ramillies">Ramillies</a> the war settled into stalemate. In 1708 the two French commanders, the Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> and the Duke of Vendome, quarrelled over their plan, and the one adopted on Louis XIV's orders was to attack <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>.</p><p>On 5 July the French suddenly moved west and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bruges">Bruges</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghent">Ghent</a>, leaving Oudenaarde as the only Allied fortress; its loss would have cut Marlborough off from the coast and from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Marlborough guessed the French plan and made a forced march that took Lessines on 10 July. He sent eleven thousand men under his quartermaster general William Cadogan to hold the main crossing of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scheldt">Scheldt</a>, and Cadogan's men built five pontoon bridges. French foragers discovered the Allied presence around 9 a.m. on 11 July. Cadogan's cavalry pushed back the French vanguard under the Duke of Biron, and Eugene crossed with twenty squadrons of Prussian cavalry.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> then launched an attack over Vendome's protests; Vendome led twelve infantry regiments himself, so neither commander directed the whole field. Half the French forces were held in reserve, and their left wing stayed inactive.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> field deputies thought Marlborough unsteady and asked Eugene to take supreme command, which he partly did, commanding the right flank. Marlborough ordered a double encirclement with 25,000 fresh Dutch troops under Lord Overkirk, who arrived at about 6 p.m. after a delay at the bridges. Sicco van Goslinga and the Prince of Orange then led some 10,000 infantry down the slope and swept aside the French infantry. Dutch cavalry cut off the retreat to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kortrijk">Kortrijk</a>, and in the dark the Allied wings briefly fired on each other before Eugene and the Prince of Orange stopped them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The French army retreated in disorder toward <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghent">Ghent</a>, its commanders blaming one another; only nightfall and some broken pontoon bridges saved it from total destruction. It lost some fourteen to fifteen thousand men, about eight thousand of them prisoners, and 25 guns, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> lost roughly three thousand. The victory restored the strategic initiative to the Allies, who besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lille">Lille</a>. The town surrendered on 22 October and the citadel yielded on 10 December. The French were driven out of almost all the Spanish Netherlands. Marlborough wrote that he and Eugene would never differ over their share of the laurels.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Oudenarde” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the War of the Spanish Succession John Churchill, Duke of Marlborough, won at Ramillies in 1706, <b>Oudenarde</b> in 1708 and Malplaquet in 1709, and the Treaty of Utrecht (1713) gave Britain Gibraltar, Minorca, Newfoundland and Nova Scotia and the asiento to supply enslaved Africans to Spanish America.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-great-britain">Kingdom of Great Britain · Military</a></li></ul></section>
